export const meta = {
  name: 'verify-key-path-refactor',
  description: 'Find and adversarially verify defects in the uncommitted refactor that removed the disproven key-schedule read',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Find', detail: 'four independent lenses over the uncommitted diff' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'one skeptic per finding, told to refute it with commands' },
    { title: 'Critique', detail: 'what the four lenses never looked at' },
  ],
}

const CONTEXT = `
The repository is at D:\\Codes\\github\\ev-reverse (Rust workspace, Windows).

BACKGROUND — the established, previously-proven facts of this project:
- EVPlayer2 is a video player whose cached .ts segments are encrypted.
- A segment's key is the 32 ASCII characters of its own hex text, used DIRECTLY as the 32 bytes of
  an AES-256 key. Decryption is: XOR the ciphertext with MD5(filename)[:16] as ASCII, then AES-256-ECB
  decrypt, then strip trailing '#' padding.
- A key is only ever known by TESTING a candidate against the segment's own encrypted bytes.
- The playback context object's 32-byte key-schedule slot (previously read at offset 0x120) NEVER
  holds a segment key. This was proven: it was read for the whole project and produced zero keys.
- A segment's key only exists in memory once the player has decrypted that segment for playback.
  Downloading a segment does NOT compute its key.

WHAT JUST CHANGED (all uncommitted — run \`git diff\` and \`git status\` to see it):
The disproven read was deleted and every one of its three consumers was re-pointed at the proven
derivation (scan the player's heap for 32-hex candidates, then test them against segment bytes):
- \`crates/evmedia-core/src/crypto.rs\`: deleted \`schedule_to_key\`, \`mix\`, \`xtime\`, \`HEAP_FILL\`.
- \`crates/evmedia-win/src/scan.rs\`: deleted \`Player::active_keys()\`.
- \`crates/evmedia-win/src/process.rs\`: deleted the \`OFF_SCHEDULE\` / \`OFF_MASK\` constants.
- \`crates/evmedia-win/src/playhead.rs\`: \`seek_window_to\` now measures the live window from
  \`segment_indexes()\` (minimum and maximum index) instead of from \`active_keys()\`.
- \`crates/evmedia-win/src/capture.rs\`: now recovers keys with \`keyscan::recover(input, candidates, empty)\`
  joined with \`segment_indexes()\`.
- \`crates/evmedia-core/src/harvest/mod.rs\`: the \`Harvester\` trait's \`keys()\` method was REPLACED by
  \`candidates()\` (a \`BTreeSet<String>\`) and a new \`indexes()\` (filename -> u32). The trait no longer
  reports keys at all; the loop derives them itself.
- \`crates/evmedia-core/src/harvest/fetch.rs\`: added \`download_missing(client, enc_dir, urls, done)\`,
  which downloads ciphertext and needs no key. \`fetch_and_decode\` now shares \`cached_cipher\`/\`plausible\`.
- \`crates/evmedia-core/src/harvest/grab.rs\`: the loop was REORDERED — it now downloads ciphertext
  first, then derives keys from the bytes on disk via \`keyscan::recover\`, then decrypts. It keeps
  two sticky maps across polls: \`known\` (filename -> key) and \`places\` (filename -> index).
- \`crates/evmedia-core/tests/grab_loop.rs\`: the fixture now implements \`candidates()\`/\`indexes()\`;
  PACKETS went from 4 to 8.

Your job is to find DEFECTS in this change. \`cargo test --workspace\` currently passes (26 tests).
`

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          file: { type: 'string', description: 'repo-relative path' },
          line: { type: 'integer' },
          claim: { type: 'string', description: 'one sentence, the defect alone' },
          detail: { type: 'string', description: 'concrete inputs/state -> wrong behaviour' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['high', 'medium', 'low'] },
        },
        required: ['file', 'claim', 'detail', 'severity'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['findings'],
}

const VERDICT = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    refuted: { type: 'boolean', description: 'true if the finding is wrong or does not matter' },
    reason: { type: 'string' },
    evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'the exact command you ran and what it printed' },
  },
  required: ['refuted', 'reason', 'evidence'],
}

const LENSES = [
  {
    key: 'loop',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR LENS: the \`grab\` loop's control flow in crates/evmedia-core/src/harvest/grab.rs.

Read the whole file, not just the diff. The loop accumulates \`known\` and \`places\` across polls and
hands \`known.keys()\` back as the skip set to \`keyscan::recover\`. Look hard for: a state where the
loop never terminates or terminates too early; a segment that is downloaded but can never be placed;
a poll where progress is reported but nothing actually advanced; the interaction between \`known\`,
\`places\`, \`ok\`, and the gap/sweep logic; what happens when \`recover\` returns a key for a file the
player has no index for; index collisions between lessons (there is no lesson key in \`grab\` at all —
is that a pre-existing limitation or did this change make it worse?).`,
  },
  {
    key: 'traces',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR LENS: leftover traces of the disproven derivation, anywhere in the repository.

Search exhaustively — the whole repo, not just crates/: Rust source, tests, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md,
README.md, CONTEXT.md, AGENTS.md, HANDOFF.md, and the Python tools under tools/parser-tools/. Look
for any remaining reference to \`active_keys\`, \`schedule_to_key\`, \`HEAP_FILL\`, \`OFF_SCHEDULE\`,
\`OFF_MASK\`, or prose that still claims a key comes from the player's key schedule / a 32-byte
schedule / "keys()". Then check the reverse: are there functions or constants that are now DEAD
because their only caller was deleted (which would now be a compile warning or an unused pub fn)?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'consumers',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR LENS: the two re-pointed consumers, and whether they still do what their own doc comments claim.

1. crates/evmedia-win/src/playhead.rs — \`seek_window_to\` now derives its window from
   \`segment_indexes()\` (min..max of the index values). Is min..max over ALL of the player's context
   objects the same quantity the old code was measuring? Does it still drive the seek loop correctly
   — will it converge, or can it oscillate? Is \`live_window\` returning None in states where it
   should not?
2. crates/evmedia-win/src/capture.rs — it now calls \`keyscan::recover(input, candidates, empty)\`.
   Check the types and the semantics line up: \`list_input_names\` vs what \`keyscan::recover\` reads,
   whether the \`input\` directory is the encrypted one, whether filenames match, and whether the
   contiguity check at the end still makes sense.
Also check both files' module doc comments against what the code now does.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'tests',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

YOUR LENS: does each test still test what its name claims?

Read crates/evmedia-core/tests/grab_loop.rs and crates/evmedia-core/tests/crypto.rs in full. The
fixture changed from reporting keys directly to reporting candidates+indexes, and PACKETS went 4 -> 8
(so SEGMENT_BYTES is 1504, while the key probe PROBE_BYTES is 752 — check that arithmetic). For each
test, ask: if the production code regressed, would this test fail? In particular, is
\`a_segment_that_cannot_be_decrypted_is_given_up_on_rather_than_retried_forever\` still exercising the
retry-and-give-up path, or does it now exercise a different path (no candidate solves the segment)?
Verify that corrupting the byte range [928, 944) really does leave the first 752 bytes intact and
really does make \`decrypt\` fail. Actually run the commands to check rather than reasoning alone.`,
  },
]

phase('Find')
const found = await parallel(
  LENSES.map((lens) => () =>
    agent(lens.prompt, { label: `find:${lens.key}`, phase: 'Find', schema: FINDINGS }).then(
      (result) => (result?.findings ?? []).map((f) => ({ ...f, lens: lens.key })),
    ),
  ),
)

const all = found.filter(Boolean).flat()
const seen = new Set()
const unique = []
for (const f of all) {
  const key = `${f.file}:${f.line ?? 0}:${f.claim.slice(0, 60)}`
  if (seen.has(key)) continue
  seen.add(key)
  unique.push(f)
}
log(`${all.length} raw finding(s), ${unique.length} unique`)
if (unique.length === 0) {
  return { verified: [], note: 'no findings from any lens' }
}

// Cap the fan-out: the cheapest way to keep this under the agent budget is to verify the most
// severe first and say plainly what was left unverified rather than dropping it silently.
const ranked = unique.sort((a, b) => {
  const order = { high: 0, medium: 1, low: 2 }
  return (order[a.severity] ?? 3) - (order[b.severity] ?? 3)
})
const CAP = 8
const toVerify = ranked.slice(0, CAP)
if (ranked.length > CAP) {
  log(`only the ${CAP} most severe of ${ranked.length} findings are being verified`)
}

phase('Verify')
const verified = await parallel(
  toVerify.map((f) => () =>
    agent(
      `${CONTEXT}

A reviewer claims the following defect in the uncommitted change. Your job is to REFUTE it. Default
to refuted=true unless you can demonstrate the defect is real.

  file: ${f.file}${f.line ? ':' + f.line : ''}
  severity: ${f.severity}
  claim: ${f.claim}
  detail: ${f.detail}

Read the actual code. Then RUN something — a test, a grep, a small cargo command, a hand computation
— that would distinguish "real defect" from "plausible-sounding but wrong". A finding that is
stylistic, speculative, or about pre-existing behaviour that this change did not introduce is
refuted. A finding that is real but cannot be triggered by any reachable input is also refuted.
Put the exact command and its output in \`evidence\`.`,
      { label: `verify:${f.lens}:${f.claim.slice(0, 34)}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT },
    ).then((v) => ({ ...f, verdict: v })),
  ),
)

const confirmed = verified
  .filter(Boolean)
  .filter((f) => f.verdict && f.verdict.refuted === false)

phase('Critique')
const critique = await agent(
  `${CONTEXT}

Four reviewers looked at this change through these lenses: ${LENSES.map((l) => l.key).join(', ')}.
They raised these findings, of which ${confirmed.length} survived an adversarial check:
${JSON.stringify(confirmed.map((f) => ({ file: f.file, claim: f.claim })), null, 1)}

Name what was NOT looked at. Specifically: which file, function, or reachable state in this change
did no lens examine? Which claim in this change is asserted by a comment but not checked by any test?
Is there a consumer of the changed trait or the deleted functions outside crates/ that nobody
grepped? Be concrete and short — a list of gaps, not a summary.`,
  { label: 'completeness-critic', phase: 'Critique' },
)

return {
  raw_findings: all.length,
  unique_findings: unique.length,
  verified_count: toVerify.length,
  unverified_count: ranked.length - toVerify.length,
  confirmed: confirmed.map((f) => ({
    file: f.file,
    line: f.line,
    severity: f.severity,
    claim: f.claim,
    detail: f.detail,
    evidence: f.verdict.evidence,
  })),
  critique,
}

