1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
2	"""Catch the instruction that stores a freshly derived segment key into a playback context.
3	
4	Ticket 07. The 32 bytes at `ctx+0x120` are the key once the player has decrypted that segment.
5	Whatever writes them is on the path to the derivation, so catching the writer's instruction pointer
6	and its backtrace is the point.
7	
8	Usage:  <python> -u probe_write.py [seconds]      (default 240)
9	
10	Preconditions, all of them load-bearing:
11	  * EVPlayer2 running, logged in, a lesson open.
12	  * That lesson must still have segments the player has NOT decrypted. The writer only runs while
13	    the player is decrypting something for the first time, so a lesson already played to the end on
14	    this machine produces nothing. Open a lesson never opened here.
15	  * Keep it playing. Do not pause.
16	
17	What the output means:
18	  "watching N page(s)"            -- the guards are on and being re-armed
19	  "access ... field=<file>"       -- an access landed inside a watched key field; if op=write, that
20	                                     is the answer to ticket 07 and the instruction is named
21	  "the writer, with context"      -- that instruction ran again; registers, stack and backtrace follow
22	  "no context with an empty key field"  -- nothing left to catch on this machine
23	
24	--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
25	How this was built, and what was measured rather than assumed
26	
27	The first version of this script armed a page guard and caught nothing in two 240-second runs. The
28	mechanism was then tested against target processes written for the purpose, and the results are why
29	this version looks the way it does:
30	
31	  1. **A page guard fires once.** It is consumed by the first access, read or write, and frida does
32	     NOT restore it. Measured: 27 target memory operations, 1 report. The first version never
33	     re-armed, so the player reading the page -- which it does constantly, it reads these context
34	     objects -- silenced the watch on the first read and the run reported nothing for the remaining
35	     240 seconds. That was the bug, and it was not "the player never wrote".
36	  2. **Re-arming from inside the access callback wedges the target.** Measured: 200 reports and the
37	     target performed zero operations for 8 seconds. Re-arming must happen from a timer.
38	  3. **Re-arming from a timer is free.** A target hammering guarded pages in a tight loop kept
39	     100.4% of its unguarded throughput at 64 and 128 pages with a 100 ms re-arm. Page count is not
40	     the hazard it was assumed to be, so this watches up to `MAX_PAGES` contexts rather than one.
41	  4. **The reported address is the faulting byte, not the start of the write.** Measured: a 32-byte
42	     store at page+0x120 reported as page+0x130. A filter that requires the address to equal the
43	     field's first byte throws the answer away, so this reports every access to a watched page and
44	     says which field, if any, the offset lands in.
45	  5. **There are no hardware watchpoints to fall back on.** `Thread` on frida 17.18.0 offers only
46	     `backtrace`; `Thread.setHardwareWatchpoint` and `Thread.setHardwareBreakpoint` are both
47	     `undefined`. The ticket's own fallback, "a per-thread breakpoint across sixteen threads", is a
48	     route that does not exist on this build.
49	  6. **A guard faults BEFORE the store executes.** The callback runs, and only when it returns does
50	     the instruction retry and complete, so reading the field inside the callback always returns the
51	     old contents. The value is therefore read on a later turn, and the first version's attempt to
52	     report it inline would have reported zeros.
53	  7. **`Thread.backtrace(..., Backtracer.ACCURATE)` raises `invalid operation`** where unwind data is
54	     missing or unusable -- it did on a plain Rust target, and this player is UPX-packed Qt, so
55	     expect it there too. FUZZY returned an empty list on the same target. A scan of the stack for
56	     words that point into a loaded module produced a real call chain where both failed, so that is
57	     what is printed when the unwind-based backtrace comes back empty.
58	  8. **`NativePointer` has no `toNumber()`** -- only `UInt64` does. The first version called it on
59	     the result of `.and()`, which raised inside the callback once per access: armed, apparently
60	     healthy, reporting nothing. `parseInt(....toString(), 16)` is the fix.
61	  9. **A reporting cap must not short-circuit the catch.** The second version stopped processing
62	     accesses once it had printed 600 of them, which on the player's pages happened within seconds
63	     -- so it sat armed, re-arming and permanently deaf. The cap now suppresses only the printing.
64	 10. **Re-arm by deferring a turn, not on a timer and never synchronously.** Against a target doing
65	     24 stores with reads in between: not re-arming caught 0; a 100 ms timer caught 23 and missed
66	     one; `setTimeout(..., 0)` from inside the callback caught **24 of 24** with the target healthy.
67	     A page the player reads continuously is mostly blind under a timer, which is what the player's
68	     own run showed: 600 accesses seen and not one of them a store.
69	
70	`watch_key.py`, the instrument this replaces, is not slow because of its 96 pages -- 128 guarded
71	pages at a 100 ms re-arm measured free. It hangs the player because it also hooks the AES site and
72	takes an ACCURATE backtrace on every hit, and that site fires once per AES round. This script does
73	not hook the AES site at all.
74	
75	This version was run end to end against a stand-in target process: six reads of the guarded page
76	(the sequence that silenced the first version), then a 32-byte store into the watched field. It
77	survived the reads, caught the store, named the instruction, dumped the registers, read the value
78	`3df51fd02753d5605cc3139e66a3561e` back correctly, and produced a call chain from the stack after
79	both backtracers came back empty. It has still not been run against EVPlayer2 itself.
80	"""
81	import frida
82	import sys
83	import time
84	
85	JS = r"""
86	'use strict';
87	var DLL = 'PlayerLibRender56_vs.dll';
88	var dll = Process.getModuleByName(DLL);
89	var EMPTY = 0xBAADF00D;      // what sits at +0x120 until a key is derived
90	var KEY_FIELD = 0x120;
91	var MAX_PAGES = 24;          // measured free up to 128; 24 keeps the log readable
92	var TICK_MS = 1000;          // safety-net re-arm only; the real re-arm is deferred per access
93	var INDIVIDUAL_CAP = 40;     // individual access lines stop after this many; hits always print
94	
95	function modOff(a) {
96	  try {
97	    var mo = Process.findModuleByAddress(a);
98	    if (!mo) return a.toString() + ' <heap-code>';
99	    return mo.name + '+0x' + a.sub(mo.base).toString(16);
100	  } catch (e) { return a.toString(); }
101	}
102	function bytesToStr(buf) {
103	  var u = new Uint8Array(buf); var s = '';
104	  for (var i = 0; i < u.length; i++) s += String.fromCharCode(u[i]);
105	  return s;
106	}
107	function stringsIn(ptrAddr, len) {
108	  var out = [];
109	  try {
110	    var u = new Uint8Array(ptrAddr.readByteArray(len));
111	    var cur = '';
112	    for (var i = 0; i < u.length; i++) {
113	      var c = u[i];
114	      if (c >= 32 && c < 127) cur += String.fromCharCode(c);
115	      else { if (cur.length >= 5) out.push(cur); cur = ''; }
116	    }
117	    if (cur.length >= 5) out.push(cur);
118	  } catch (e) {}
119	  return out;
120	}
121	function readStr(obj, off) {
122	  try {
123	    var f = obj.add(off);
124	    var n = f.add(16).readU64().toNumber();
125	    var c = f.add(24).readU64().toNumber();
126	    if (n < 0 || n > 8192 || c < n || c > 1048576) return null;
127	    if (n === 0) return '';
128	    var data = (c < 16) ? f.readByteArray(n) : f.readPointer().readByteArray(n);
129	    return data ? bytesToStr(data) : null;
130	  } catch (e) { return null; }
131	}
132	
133	// A backtrace that does not need unwind data: walk the stack for words that point into a loaded
134	// module. `Thread.backtrace(..., Backtracer.ACCURATE)` raises `invalid operation` where the unwind
135	// tables are missing or unusable -- measured on a plain Rust target, and this player is UPX-packed
136	// Qt, so expect it there too. This always answers, at the cost of false positives.
137	function stackReturnAddrs(sp, len) {
138	  var out = [];
139	  for (var off = 0; off < len; off += 8) {
140	    try {
141	      var p = sp.add(off).readPointer();
142	      if (p.isNull()) continue;
143	      var mo = Process.findModuleByAddress(p);
144	      if (mo) out.push('[rsp+' + off.toString(16) + '] ' + mo.name + '+0x' + p.sub(mo.base).toString(16));
145	    } catch (e) {}
146	  }
147	  return out;
148	}
149	
150	// The playback contexts: objects whose vtable sits in the DLL's vtable range, carrying a
151	// 32-character mask at +0x268 and a .ts name at +0x18. One-off, done before any guard is armed --
152	// the scan reads these pages, and reading a guarded page raises.
153	function findContexts() {
154	  var lo = dll.base.add(0x802000), hi = dll.base.add(0x804000);
155	  var hex = lo.toString().slice(2); while (hex.length < 16) hex = '0' + hex;
156	  var b = []; for (var i = 0; i < 8; i++) b.push(hex.slice(14 - i * 2, 16 - i * 2));
157	  var pat = ['??', '??', b[2], b[3], b[4], b[5], b[6], b[7]].join(' ');
158	  var objs = [];
159	  Process.enumerateRanges('rw-').forEach(function (r) {
160	    if (r.size > 0x10000000) return;
161	    try {
162	      Memory.scanSync(r.base, r.size, pat).forEach(function (m) {
163	        var obj = m.address;
164	        var mask = readStr(obj, 0x268);
165	        if (!mask || mask.length !== 32) return;
166	        var file = readStr(obj, 0x18);
167	        if (!file || file.slice(-3) !== '.ts') return;
168	        var index = obj.add(8).readU32();
169	        if (index > 20_000_000) return;
170	        objs.push({ obj: obj, file: file, index: index,
171	                    empty: obj.add(KEY_FIELD).readU32() === EMPTY });
172	      });
173	    } catch (e) {}
174	  });
175	  return objs;
176	}
177	
178	var watched = [];     // { page, fieldOff, file, index, ctx }
179	var ranges = [];
180	var reports = 0;
181	var found = false;
182	var timer = null;
183	var instrHooked = false;
184	var writerDumps = 0;
185	var ticks = 0;
186	
187	function chooseTargets() {
188	  var objs = findContexts();
189	  var empty = objs.filter(function (o) { return o.empty; });
190	  send({t: 'log', msg: 'found ' + objs.length + ' context(s), ' + empty.length +
191	        ' with an empty key field'});
192	  if (!empty.length) {
193	    send({t: 'nodice', msg: 'no context with an empty key field. The writer only runs for a segment ' +
194	          'the player has not decrypted yet -- open a lesson that has not been played on this machine.'});
195	    return null;
196	  }
197	
198	  // Aim just past what has already been decrypted: the player decrypts roughly in playhead order, so
199	  // the lowest indexes still empty are the ones about to be filled. An arbitrary context, which is
200	  // what the first version guarded, is most likely one the player will never touch again.
201	  var keyed = objs.filter(function (o) { return !o.empty; })
202	                  .map(function (o) { return o.index; });
203	  var frontier = keyed.length ? Math.max.apply(null, keyed) : -1;
204	  send({t: 'log', msg: keyed.length + ' of ' + objs.length + ' context(s) hold a key; the highest ' +
205	        'decrypted index is ' + frontier});
206	  if (keyed.length <= 2) {
207	    send({t: 'log', msg: '  that is very few. The writer only runs while the player decrypts a ' +
208	          'segment it has not decrypted on this machine before -- if this lesson has already been ' +
209	          'played here, nothing will be written and this run cannot catch anything. Open a lesson ' +
210	          'never played on this machine and keep it playing.'});
211	  }
212	  var byIndex = function (a, b) { return a.index - b.index; };
213	  var ahead = empty.filter(function (o) { return o.index > frontier; }).sort(byIndex);
214	  var chosen = (ahead.length ? ahead : empty.sort(byIndex)).slice(0, MAX_PAGES);
215	  send({t: 'log', msg: 'aiming at ' + chosen.length + ' context(s), index ' +
216	        chosen[0].index + '..' + chosen[chosen.length - 1].index});
217	
218	  var pages = {};
219	  chosen.forEach(function (o) {
220	    var field = o.obj.add(KEY_FIELD);
221	    var pg = field.and(ptr('0xfffffffffffff000')).toString();
222	    pages[pg] = { page: pg, fieldOff: parseInt(field.and(ptr('0xfff')).toString(), 16),
223	                  file: o.file, index: o.index, ctx: o.obj.toString() };
224	  });
225	  watched = Object.keys(pages).map(function (k) { return pages[k]; });
226	  ranges = Object.keys(pages).map(function (k) { return { base: ptr(k), size: 0x1000 }; });
227	  return ranges;
228	}
229	
230	function fieldAt(pageStr, off) {
231	  for (var i = 0; i < watched.length; i++) {
232	    var w = watched[i];
233	    if (w.page === pageStr && off >= w.fieldOff && off < w.fieldOff + 32) return w;
234	  }
235	  return null;
236	}
